A||||7||Brian84SE||Cooling system diagnosis||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||03-06-2000||07:50 PM||||Here are the symptoms (4 cyl)<BR>System is normal(a bit below the 220 line) when idling driving around town, etc.<P>The problem is when I go on the highway (60-65).  It climbs to 230+. BUT, I turn the air cond. on (causing the cooling fan to kick in) and it IMMEDIATELY goes down below 220.<BR>I have bled the system.  The timing is good.  No leaks that I can see.  Havent done any warm  weather driving yet.  This is all 30-50 degrees ambient temperature.  Any ideas?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||03-06-2000||07:58 PM||||Sounds to me like the thermostat isn't opening completely. Higher rpms, more heat, but the coolant flow is restricted through the thermostat.  You can try putting it in a pot on the stove, and see what temp it opens at, or try another one and see if it solves the problem. Make sure you have the right temp (195) thermostat in there too. I like to put a 180 in in the hotter summer months, but I do a lot of city stop and go driving too. I believe the 180 'stats have a higher flow rate too.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian84SE||03-06-2000||08:13 PM||||I had given thought to a clogged radiator.  But, it would run hot all the time wouldnt it?  And running the fan wouldnt bring such a  drastic change in temperature<P>It is a new thermostat.  Perhaps it's defective?  I'll pick up a 180 degree one tommorrow.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-06-2000||08:17 PM||||I didn't think of clogged radiator. That is a possibilty, it would create a bottleneck in the system. Also make sure the coolant pipes that run the lenght of the car on both sides are in good shape too.  If you want, you could try running without the 'stat for a few miles and see what the temp does. If the radiator is clogging the temp should still rise.<P>I just re-read your original post.  You said temp "IMMEDIATLY" drops.  Sounds to me like your guage might be lying.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdm1964||03-06-2000||08:30 PM||||I had the same prob, but with a v-6. New thermostat,water pump, belts, hoses, rad rotted out. It still runs the same temp you are. a little below 220, goes to about 230 in town, stop and go, i have not had it back on the freeway yet, But after 220, the guage seems to just go back and forth about 10 degrees. driving thru atlanta last month the temp guage just shot to full overheat, but the light did not come on, pulled over no water pouring out, cooled it down , rad is full, so i guess the sending unit might be fixing to go out. I will try the 180 therostat.It gets real hot in georgia, I want a good cooling system.||||reg||14
Z||000005||fiero56||03-06-2000||08:35 PM||||Have you guys ever considered putting in a manual cooling fan switch. I wired one under my dash. While wating in heavy traffic or really slow fast food lines, I just flip the switch, and the fan comes on. Takes under 5 minutes to wire one up.<P><BR>The coolant idiot light and the guage get their signals from different sensors.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Brian84SE||03-06-2000||10:07 PM||||I will check the pipes to make sure theyre not crushed. Yes, it is a little strange that the temperature goes down so quick with turning on the fan.  But I do believe the guage because it starts to ping pretty good when it gets hot. (EGR is working)||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||03-06-2000||10:18 PM||||temp raises when driving highway speed........ <P>get under it and make sure something isn't blocking the radiator as well. see if you can either loosen the condenser brackets ot the radiator brakets and look between them. If some vermin got in there and dragged in a nest it could also cause this.<P>If problem definatly sounds like either a water flow problem or an air flow problem.||||reg||1
